out.push(SAMPLE(['Mrs Tarabishi\'s first response letter had eleven points and eleven concessions, four hundred words of accepted, accepted, accepted, and it came back for a third round with six new points. Mrs Khoury said of course it did: a letter that accepts everything tells an editor that the author has no view about their own paper, and an editor who learns that sends it to a third referee. Dr Qabbani said that is not what any of them was taught, and Mrs Tarabishi said it is the opposite of what she was taught, which was to be grateful — and two of the eleven she did not agree with at all, and she made the changes anyway, and the chapter is worse in both places, and she now has to defend two sentences she does not believe in a viva. Mrs Oueidat said a pleading that admits everything is withdrawn and not answered. Mr Shihabi asked what the shape is, and numbered, in the referee\'s order, with the same numbers they used; each one labelled accepted or not accepted in those two words; a page reference for every change; at least one reasoned refusal; and not a syllable about anybody\'s tone. Mrs Khoury said the page reference is the half nobody writes and the only half an editor uses, because an editor with fourteen papers does not find the change, they ask the referee to find it, which is a fourth round. Mr Tayara asked whether one refusal is a rule, and it is not a rule and it is almost always available: eleven points from two readers of one paper will contain at least one you can answer, and a letter with one reasoned refusal reads as the work of somebody who has read their own paper. Mrs Ajouz said hers is a registration officer and there is only one of him, and the shape is identical and the stakes are higher because he can refuse the file. Mrs Rizk asked how long, and two hundred and sixty to three hundred words for eleven points, about twenty-four words a point. Mr Attar asked why the tone matters so much, and because the editor is your reader and the referee is not. Mr Zahreddine said he writes the same letter to insurers four times a year and nobody has ever taught it. And Mrs Tarabishi said the thing she will take away is the two sentences she does not believe: she conceded them to be agreeable and they are in the chapter, and the viva will ask her about them because they are the weakest things in it, and the referee who asked for them will not be in the room.']));
