out.push(A('**1** _It is not possible to say what the uptake will be._ ⚠ **Delete _that_.**   **2** _…in all but one **position**._   **3** _We are unable to exclude **that** the single reading is a sample position._   **4** _…and therefore **establishes** a difference and not a date._   **5** _You are right that one month is a short period. **The finding is a count** and does not depend on the month._ ⚠ **Delete _however_: the next sentence begins with the finding.**   **6** _Referee 2, point 5: not **accepted**, and the reason is **at** page 14._'));
out.push(S('Stage 2 · Listen and read'));
out.push(A('**b** ⚠ **The editor, and only the editor.** **She is the one who has to do something by Friday**, and the author and the referee have each written to the person who agrees with them, which is why the file is three weeks old. **The letter gives her the two questions, the answer to the one that has an answer with its page, the two readings of the one that has not, the repair with its word count, and both branches.**'));
out.push(NOTE('⚠ **This is the ninth mediation and the second in which the question put to you is refused outright, and the difference from 8.7 is the whole of why it is here: the person refused in 8.7 had a ranking to publish, and the person refused here has to decide something on Friday.** **A refusal with nothing beside it is the answer Mrs Tarabishi gave first and it was no use on Friday.** **Four findings and one request is a decision, and a reader handed a decision stops asking for an adjudication.** ⚠ **Say it flat.** _I have no basis for preferring one of them to the other._ **Stress on _no_ is a refusal and the decider hears evasion; flat, it is a statement of what the evidence will carry** — **and the sentence after it has to be the request or the branch, immediately, or the flatness is wasted.** **Drill the two sentences as a pair and not as one.**'));
out.push(NOTE('**Both branches, and this is the part a class will leave out because it feels like indecision.** ⚠ **It is the opposite.** **A mediator who gives one branch has taken the decision and hidden it**, and the decider finds out in the next round that there was a branch — and then distrusts the branch they were given. **Insist on two one-line branches at 4a and check them at 4b by asking the partner what they would do on Friday.** ⚠ **And where there genuinely is only one branch, as on Pair B\'s card, the instruction is to say so expressly**: _there is one course and not two._ **A decider given two branches where one exists takes the one that does not work**, which is the fault this cycle produces when it is taught as a formula.'));
