out.push(SAMPLE(['Asked who gets the letter, Mrs Khoury said the editor and only the editor, because she is the one who has to do something by Friday, and the author and the referee have each written to the person who agrees with them, which is why it is now three weeks old. Mrs Tarabishi said she wrote to the editor, and Mrs Khoury said she wrote to the referee through the editor, which is the commonest fault in this cycle: point 5 cannot be done is an answer to him, and an editor who is not an economist reads it and has two assertions and no way of choosing. Dr Qabbani asked what she needs, and Mrs Oueidat said not an opinion about who is right but a decision she can take, which means the two questions named and one line of what follows from each answer. Mr Shihabi asked what the two questions are here, and can the comparison be made, which is the author\'s, and the answer is no and page 18 says so; and is the paper\'s central claim supported without it, which is the referee\'s, and the answer depends entirely on whether the claim is read in the abstract or in the chapter. Mrs Khoury said that is the whole of it and nobody had seen it: the abstract claims a rate that cannot be placed against anything and the chapter claims a rate and says at page 18 that it cannot be placed, so the referee is right about the abstract and the author is right about the chapter, and they are each defending the document they wrote last. Mrs Ajouz said so the repair is in the abstract, and eleven words in the abstract and the referee\'s objection has nowhere to stand, which is 9.5, and Mrs Tarabishi had been sent the same finding twice in one unit and did not recognise it the second time. Mr Tayara asked whether the editor needs to be told all of that, and she needs four sentences and no history, and then one line saying what to do if she accepts the repair and one if she does not. Mrs Rizk asked what you do not write, and Mrs Oueidat said that the referee has misread the paper, because he has not: he read the abstract, which is what he was sent first and what the editor read too. Mr Attar asked what happens if the author will not change the abstract, and then the editor has a different decision and should be told which one. Mr Zahreddine said an insurer reads the covering letter and not the opinion. And Mrs Tarabishi said the thing she will take away is which document she was defending: she answered the referee about page 18 four times and the sentence he was objecting to was on page 3, and he was right every time he wrote to her.']));
